<div class="wrap">
  <header class="masthead">
    <div class="eyebrow">Migration analysis · Drivetrain data platform · rev 2</div>
    <h1>Replacing the Meltano taps with faucet-stream</h1>
    <p class="lede">Can faucet-stream drop into <code>DrivetrainAi/meltano_taps</code> — 46 sources into one BigQuery target — and produce the same raw tables? Scored under your rules: <strong>ignore <code>_sdc_</code> columns</strong>, count <strong>inbuilt transforms</strong> and the <strong>SQL transform</strong>, and <strong>never count WASM</strong>.</p>
    <p class="meta"><b>Rules applied:</b> _sdc_* dropped · column naming/flatten via inbuilt transforms counts · SQL transform counts (and is flagged) · WASM does not count · <b>sink:</b> faucet-sink-bigquery · <b>goal:</b> in-place replace, same BigQuery tables · Singer excluded — verdicts use each tap's real transport.</p>
  </header>

  <div class="band">
    <div class="stat ok"><div class="n">20</div><div class="l">Supported</div><div class="d">Stock connector + inbuilt transforms only.</div></div>
    <div class="stat work"><div class="n">10</div><div class="l">Needs SQL</div><div class="d">The DuckDB SQL transform unlocks it — flagged below.</div></div>
    <div class="stat part"><div class="n">12</div><div class="l">Partial</div><div class="d">Auth gap, or report streams coupled to BigQuery.</div></div>
    <div class="stat block"><div class="n">4</div><div class="l">Blocked</div><div class="d">Bulk / SDK / SOAP-XLSX / runtime streams.</div></div>
  </div>

  <section>
    <div class="callout good">
      <h3>✓ With <code>_sdc_</code> dropped and naming handled in the source, the sink is largely a solved problem</h3>
      <p>faucet-sink-bigquery already loads via <strong>BigQuery load jobs with <code>create_disposition: CREATE_IF_NEEDED</code></strong>, does <strong>overwrite = staged truncate-swap preserving partitioning/clustering</strong>, <strong>MERGE upsert</strong>, and <strong>schema evolution</strong> (<code>ALTER … ADD COLUMN</code>). The z3z1ma-specific machinery you told me to ignore — the six <code>_sdc_*</code> columns — is gone from the requirement.</p>
      <p>The rest of z3z1ma's "denormalized" shape is reproducible <strong>on the source side with inbuilt transforms</strong>: <code>flatten</code> → <code>parent_child</code> columns, <code>keys_case</code> → snake_case, <code>rename_keys</code>/<code>rename_field</code> for aliases, <code>set</code> for injected columns (company/env/parent-ids), <code>cast</code> for epoch↔timestamp. So <strong>column naming and flattening are no longer blockers.</strong></p>
      <p><strong>Two small sink follow-ups remain</strong> (not blockers): match z3z1ma's type-mapping rules (e.g. <code>integer+string → STRING</code>, JSON fallback for schemaless objects), and expose partition/cluster config (today it preserves a pre-created table's spec). And one <em>ubiquitous</em> shaping step — JSON-stringifying a nested field — has no inbuilt transform yet, so it currently falls to SQL; a one-line <code>json_encode</code> transform would keep those rows inbuilt-only (see §04).</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2><span class="num">01</span>Per-source parity matrix</h2>
    <p class="sub">All 46 taps, re-scored. <strong>Supported</strong> = stock connector + inbuilt transforms. <strong>Needs SQL</strong> = the DuckDB transform is required (and is fine by your rules) — the exact reshape is named. <strong>Partial</strong>/<strong>Blocked</strong> are <em>not</em> shaping problems — they're auth, transport, dynamic-stream, or destination-coupling gaps that neither SQL nor WASM would fix.</p>
    <div class="filters" id="filters" role="group" aria-label="Filter by verdict">
      <button class="chip" data-k="all" aria-pressed="true">All · 46</button>
      <button class="chip" data-k="ok" aria-pressed="false"><span class="dot"></span>Supported · 20</button>
      <button class="chip" data-k="work" aria-pressed="false"><span class="dot"></span>Needs SQL · 10</button>
      <button class="chip" data-k="part" aria-pressed="false"><span class="dot"></span>Partial · 12</button>
      <button class="chip" data-k="block" aria-pressed="false"><span class="dot"></span>Blocked · 4</button>
    </div>
    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr>
          <th>Source</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Auth</th><th>Fan-out / streams</th><th>Verdict</th><th>What decides it</th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody id="rows"></tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="footnote">Every row also assumes the ubiquitous "stringify nested field → STRING column" step is available (via SQL today, or the proposed <code>json_encode</code> inbuilt transform). "Partial" report streams: a faucet <code>upsert</code>/overwrite can produce a <em>correct</em> table, but matching the tap's exact historical accumulation depends on its BigQuery delete-window behaviour.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2><span class="num">02</span>What the SQL transform unlocks</h2>
    <p class="sub">You asked specifically what SQL buys. These are the reshapes no inbuilt transform does, but DuckDB (reference tables + recursive CTEs + pivot/unpivot) does — so they count as supported, flagged:</p>
    <div class="cards">
      <div class="card"><h3>Wide → long unpivot</h3><ul>
        <li><b>deel</b> — gross-to-net dynamic column map → tall rows</li>
        <li><b>sharepoint / onedrive</b> — timeseries period-header sheet → long</li>
        <li><b>personio</b> — v1 custom-report attribute pivot</li>
      </ul></div>
      <div class="card"><h3>Reference-table joins</h3><ul>
        <li><b>hibob</b> — named-list ID → human-readable label</li>
        <li><b>anthropic</b> — <code>api_key_costs</code> priced against a rate table</li>
        <li><b>bamboohr</b> — <code>/meta/fields</code> field aliasing</li>
      </ul></div>
      <div class="card"><h3>Report-tree / matrix flatten</h3><ul>
        <li><b>rillet</b> — balance-sheet tree → leaf rows (heaviest)</li>
        <li><b>quickbooks / xero</b> — Rows/Columns report matrix → rows</li>
        <li><b>attio</b> — value-map → one row per (record, attribute)</li>
        <li><b>ukg_pro</b> — job × compensation × employment cross-join</li>
      </ul></div>
    </div>
    <p class="footnote">A tiny inbuilt <code>json_encode</code>/<code>stringify</code> transform would move the ~everyone "stringify nested" case out of SQL entirely; only the genuine reshapes above would then need SQL.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2><span class="num">03</span>What only WASM could do — so it does <em>not</em> count</h2>
    <p class="sub">Per your rule, anything reachable only via WASM is left uncounted. Across all 46 taps there is essentially one such case:</p>
    <div class="cards">
      <div class="card"><h3><span class="tag no">not counted</span> Binary file decode in-stream</h3><ul>
        <li><b>oracle_fusion</b> returns a <b>base64-encoded XLSX inside a SOAP body</b>; reproducing it means base64-decode → parse an XLSX binary mid-pipeline. SQL can't; only arbitrary code (WASM) could — so it stays <b>Blocked</b>.</li>
      </ul></div>
      <div class="card"><h3>Not a transform problem at all</h3><ul>
        <li><b>Custom request signing</b> (skyslope HMAC) and <b>multi-step session login</b> (sap, acumatica, bullhorn, sage) happen at <em>request</em> time — neither SQL nor WASM touches them. They need an <b>auth provider</b>, not a transform (§04 item 1).</li>
        <li><b>Async job APIs</b> (salesforce bulk, stripe reports) and <b>runtime stream synthesis</b> (hubspot) are transport/planning gaps — again outside transform scope.</li>
      </ul></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2><span class="num">04</span>What to build for full parity</h2>
    <p class="sub">Re-ordered for the new rules — the real unlocks are source-side. Each is a first-class framework primitive that unlocks a <em>class</em> of connectors, now filed as tracked epics (#510–#518, under roadmap #38).</p>
    <ol class="build">
      <li><h3>Session / multi-step / signing auth provider <span class="who">— #511 · unlocks sap · acumatica · bullhorn · syteline · sage · skyslope</span></h3><p>A provider that runs a login pre-flight (capturing a token / cookie / dynamic base-URL) and can compute a per-request signature (custom HMAC). Modelled on the existing <code>oauth2_refresh</code> provider. Biggest single source-side unlock.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Bookmark → request-parameter injection <span class="who">— #513 · server-side incremental for many taps</span></h3><p>Flow the stored bookmark into a query param / filter (OData <code>$filter</code>, Lucene range, <code>gte|</code>, <code>If-Modified-Since</code>). Today faucet filters incremental client-side; several taps need the server-side form to match row counts.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Inbuilt <code>json_encode</code> transform <span class="who">— part of #516 · removes the SQL dependency for most rows</span></h3><p>One transform that JSON-stringifies a nested field to a STRING column. Cheap, and it moves the ubiquitous "stringify nested" step out of SQL, keeping ~most Needs-SQL rows inbuilt-only.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Async-job source pattern <span class="who">— #514 + #515 · salesforce bulk · stripe reports</span></h3><p>A source shape that submits a job, polls, and downloads the result (CSV). Larger effort; only two taps genuinely require it.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Report-stream decoupling recipe <span class="who">— #518 · quickbooks · xero · zohobooks · sage report streams</span></h3><p>These taps <code>DELETE</code> a rolling window from BigQuery then re-append. Provide a keyed-<code>upsert</code> / partition-overwrite recipe so the destination converges without the connector reaching into the warehouse. (Correct table; byte-identical history depends on the window semantics.)</p></li>
      <li><h3>Optional <code>_faucet_*</code> metadata columns <span class="who">— #510 · your idea, filed</span></h3><p>Not required for parity (you dropped <code>_sdc</code>), but a good faucet-native lineage feature: an opt-in sink decorator stamping <code>_faucet_extracted_at</code> / <code>_faucet_loaded_at</code> / <code>_faucet_run_id</code> / <code>_faucet_source</code>. Filed as an enhancement.</p></li>
      <li><h3>BigQuery type-mapping + partition/cluster options <span class="who">— sink polish (also #512 OData source, #516 unpivot/lookup, #517 discovery)</span></h3><p>Match z3z1ma's type rules (integer+string→STRING, JSON fallback) and let the sink set time-partition + clustering on load. Minor.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2><span class="num">05</span>Suggested rollout</h2>
    <p class="sub">Roughly two-thirds of the fleet is reachable now with connectors + transforms; the tail is gated by the auth/transport work above.</p>
    <div class="cards">
      <div class="card"><h3><span class="v ok">Wave 1</span> Supported — 20</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;margin:.2rem 0 .5rem">Stock connector + inbuilt transforms. Mostly full-refresh <code>overwrite</code> — easiest to prove identical.</p><ul>
        <li>ramp, brex, invoiced, redshift, ms365fno, ms365bc, netsuite_pro, afas, dualentry, deskera, restaurant365, campfire, adp, isolved, paylocity, rippling, greenhouse, lever, navan, openai</li>
      </ul></div>
      <div class="card"><h3><span class="v work">Wave 2</span> Needs SQL — 10</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;margin:.2rem 0 .5rem">Same, plus one DuckDB SQL transform for the named reshape. Fine by your rules.</p><ul>
        <li>anthropic, sharepointsites, onedrive, bamboohr, hibob, personio, ukg_pro, deel, attio, rillet</li>
      </ul></div>
      <div class="card"><h3><span class="v part">Wave 3</span> Partial — 12</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;margin:.2rem 0 .5rem">Need the session/signing auth provider, or their report streams are BigQuery-coupled (entities migrate now).</p><ul>
        <li>sap, syteline, acumatica, shopify, plaid, bullhorn, skyslope, netsuite, quickbooks*, xero*, zohobooks*, sage* <span style="color:var(--faint)">(*entity streams now; report streams need decoupling)</span></li>
      </ul></div>
      <div class="card"><h3><span class="v block">Hold</span> Blocked — 4</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;margin:.2rem 0 .5rem">Keep on Meltano until the async-job pattern lands, or accept a non-identical rebuild.</p><ul>
        <li>salesforce (bulk API), stripe (SDK + events + reports), hubspot (runtime streams + BigQuery side-channel), oracle_fusion (SOAP + XLSX)</li>
      </ul></div>
    </div>
  </section>

